think Designed compose-not-braid: the module OWNS only the argmin and its margin; shape_match owns the metric, hu_moments the fingerprints, label the LabelMap. Template{class_id, hu}; Match{index, class_id, distance, margin}; classify + classify_oriented; bounded by shape_classify_max_templates (256); ties to the earliest template (strict < never replaces an equal incumbent) so the choice is deterministic.
obs First metal run tripped PlusMarginNotConfident -- my margin>1.0 for the plus was memory, not metal. Probed the real pairwise distances: plus->block 0.18, plus->L 10.1, tri->all 14+. A symmetric plus and a solid block are near-twins to Hu moments (both compact, odd invariants zero). Kept the honest fact rather than fighting it.
obs Reframed the crux keyed to metal: the confident-margin test now uses the chiral L (nearest other class ~10 away), and the plus-vs-block near-collision (0.18) became an explicit THIN-MARGIN demonstration -- the margin surfacing near-twins is exactly its purpose, a stronger and more honest teaching than pretending Hu moments separate every shape.
obs The decisive oriented-vs-blind crux stands: with a chiral shape AND its mirror as two classes, the reflection-blind classifier reads a mirror mark as indistinguishable from both (margin under 1e-8) while classify_oriented names the correct handed class (101) a real margin away -- the seventh Hu invariant's sign-flip surfacing as the right label, the payoff of the oriented metric inside a classifier.
